{"id": "2026-10-03-news-council-of-states-backs-eu-treaty-package", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "Council of States backs EU treaty package", "summary": "The Council of States approved the EU treaty package in principle after a 24-hour debate but added a double-majority hurdle, and in the same session tightened UBS capital rules and renewed the Epidemics Act.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/session-in-zahlen-24-stunden-16-milliarden-43-zu-1-so-lief-die-herbstsession", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "After a marathon debate of some 24 hours, the Council of States (Ständerat — parliament's smaller chamber, representing the cantons) approved the EU treaty package (\"Bilaterals III\") in principle on Friday 2 October, but bolted on hurdles that make eventual approval at the ballot box harder. The chamber backed entering into the deal yet decided it must clear a double majority — of both the popular vote and the cantons (the \"Ständemehr\") — rather than a simple majority of voters, a procedural bar several commentators read as a vote of no confidence in the package itself. On the safeguard clause governing free movement, senators adopted a proposal from Esther Friedli (SVP/UDC) requiring EU citizens to produce a criminal-record extract when moving to Switzerland; on wage protection they sided with the Federal Council's contested dismissal-protection compromise. The same session produced two further notable decisions: the chamber voted to require UBS — the country's last big bank after the 2023 Credit Suisse rescue — to back its foreign subsidiaries with 90% hard capital (up from 45% today), a tightening UBS says in a statement would cost it about 16 billion dollars; and it renewed the Epidemics Act 43 votes to 1, drawing lessons from Covid while writing in restraint on school closures, home-office orders and vaccine mandates. The National Council (the larger chamber) takes up all three files, starting with the EU package, in December.", "why": "for a reader in Switzerland, Bilaterals III is the single biggest question hanging over EU–CH relations — free movement, market access and the right to work — and the Council of States' double-majority decision raises the hurdle any eventual popular vote would have to clear.", "thread_id": "2026-09-04-news-committee-backs-eu-treaty-keeps-double-majority", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-04", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "European Union", "Council of States", "UBS", "Bilaterals III"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-electric-cars-pass-a-third-of-swiss-sales", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "Electric cars pass a third of Swiss sales", "summary": "Fully electric cars made up 35.7% of September new-car registrations in Switzerland for the first time, with China's BYD surging, Auto-Suisse figures show.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/du-jamais-vu-les-voitures-electriques-franchissent-la-barre-du-tiers-des-ventes-en-suisse-portees-par-l-offensive-chinoise-de-byd", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Fully electric cars crossed a third of new-car registrations in Switzerland for the first time in September, reaching 35.7% of the month's sales, figures from the importers' federation Auto-Suisse published on Friday 2 October show. A year earlier the share was 25.9%; across the first nine months of 2026, battery-electric vehicles made up 26.2% of sales, up from 21.1% in the same period of 2025. Driving the shift is a wave of Chinese marques: BYD multiplied its Swiss registrations roughly ninefold in a year and now trails Tesla closely on the Swiss market, while record pump prices for petrol add to the incentive to switch.", "why": "passing a third of monthly sales is a visible tipping point in how fast Switzerland's car fleet is electrifying — and a measure of how quickly Chinese manufacturers are reshaping a market long dominated by European and US brands.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-electric-cars-pass-a-third-of-swiss-sales", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "BYD", "Tesla", "Auto-Suisse"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-federal-council-funds-eight-ukraine-projects", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "Federal Council funds eight Ukraine projects", "summary": "The Swiss Federal Council approved 135 million francs for eight Swiss-company reconstruction projects in Ukraine.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/4XKhPgvW0rYD", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Council decided on Friday 2 October to fund eight Swiss-company projects for Ukraine's reconstruction, committing 135 million francs across the energy, public-transport and water sectors. The projects were chosen through a competitive tender among Swiss firms active in Ukraine, under the Swiss Ukraine Programme 2025–2028; each company must cover at least 10% of its project's cost, and the final selection was made jointly with the Ukrainian government against criteria including development impact, technical feasibility and \"Swissness.\"", "why": "it channels Swiss industrial expertise into Ukraine's war-damaged infrastructure while keeping Swiss firms engaged in the country's rebuilding.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-federal-council-funds-eight-ukraine-projects", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Ukraine", "Federal Council"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-federal-court-clarifies-family-reunification-dea", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "Federal Court clarifies family-reunification deadline", "summary": "Switzerland's Federal Supreme Court ruled the family-reunification deadline starts at legal recognition of paternity, letting a naturalised Swiss man bring his daughter from Gambia.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/urteil-zum-familiennachzug-tochter-darf-in-die-schweiz-weil-der-vater-nichts-von-ihr-wusste", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Supreme Court (Bundesgericht, Switzerland's highest court) ruled on Friday 2 October that a naturalised Swiss man may bring his out-of-wedlock daughter from Gambia to live with him, clarifying when the deadline for family reunification begins to run. Zurich authorities had refused the girl a residence permit, arguing the father had missed a five-year window to apply. A majority of the court disagreed: the deadline begins only once paternity is legally recognised (here, abroad), and even had it lapsed, the case presented \"important family reasons\" for a late reunification — the court noted the girl has no genuine care alternative in Gambia.", "why": "the judgment sets a precedent on a contested point of Swiss immigration law — which event starts the reunification clock — that will shape comparable cases.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-federal-court-clarifies-family-reunification-dea", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Federal Supreme Court", "Gambia"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-g7-agrees-100-million-barrel-oil-and-diesel-rele", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "G7 agrees 100-million-barrel oil and diesel release", "summary": "G7 leaders agreed a coordinated IEA release of 100 million barrels of oil and diesel over four months to ease soaring prices.", "url": "https://www.iea.org/news/executive-director-participates-in-g7-leaders-meeting-on-energy-security-and-markets", "source_domain": "iea.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Group of Seven agreed on Friday 2 October to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels of oil and diesel through the International Energy Agency (IEA — the rich democracies' energy-security body) over the next four months, including a substantial diesel release in the first 20 days, to tame soaring fuel prices. The decision came out of a G7 leaders' video meeting chaired by French President Emmanuel Macron — France holds the 2026 G7 presidency — and attended by, among others, US President Donald Trump, Germany's Friedrich Merz, Canada's Mark Carney, Italy's Giorgia Meloni and European Commission chief Ursula von der Leyen. The IEA said about 325 million barrels of a separate 400-million-barrel emergency release agreed on 11 March have already been drawn down — more than 80% — to plug the supply gap from the Strait of Hormuz crisis (the chokepoint for Gulf oil exports, disrupted by the US-Israel war on Iran). Diesel remains the tightest: Ukrainian strikes have halted Russian refinery exports and China has stopped exporting diesel, pushing the average US price to a record of about 6.50 dollars a gallon last week.", "why": "diesel moves freight, farming and heating across Europe, so a drawdown aimed squarely at the diesel crunch bears directly on transport and energy costs — though releasing reserves treats the symptom, not the two wars driving the supply shocks behind it.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-g7-agrees-100-million-barrel-oil-and-diesel-rele", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["G7", "IEA", "European Union", "Strait of Hormuz"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-north-korea-fires-ballistic-missile", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "North Korea fires ballistic missile", "summary": "North Korea launched a ballistic missile toward the sea on Saturday after rejecting Seoul's demand for an apology over DMZ mine blasts.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/03/north-korea-launches-ballistic-missile-after-seoul-demands-apology-over-wounded-soldiers", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "North Korea fired a ballistic missile toward the sea on Saturday 3 October, South Korea and Japan said, a day after Pyongyang rejected Seoul's demand for an apology over mine blasts that wounded South Korean soldiers in the Demilitarized Zone (DMZ — the heavily fortified buffer dividing the two Koreas). The missile launched from the Wonsan area at about 6:30am and flew more than 700 km (about 434 miles) before coming down, according to South Korea's military, which convened an emergency national-security meeting. The launch followed a Friday statement by Kim Yo-jong, sister of leader Kim Jong-un, who dismissed President Lee Jae-myung's call for dialogue as \"the climax of the farce\" and warned the North was \"ready for anything.\" Seoul says North Korean mines caused the blast that wounded three soldiers on a reconnaissance mission inside the 4-km-wide zone. It is at least the eleventh North Korean missile test this year.", "why": "the test hardens an escalating exchange of threats just as Lee's government tries to reopen dialogue, signalling that Pyongyang will answer political pressure with weapons rather than talks.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-north-korea-fires-ballistic-missile", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["North Korea", "South Korea", "Kim Yo-jong"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-dr-congo-ebola-toll-passes-4-000", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "DR Congo Ebola toll passes 4,000", "summary": "The Ebola outbreak in DR Congo has killed more than 4,000 people, the deadliest in the country's history, authorities say.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/l-epidemie-d-ebola-depasse-les-4000-morts-en-republique-du-congo", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["health", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) has killed more than 4,000 people, Congolese authorities reported on Friday 2 October, making it the deadliest in the country's history. The latest tally — 8,300 cases, 4,018 deaths and 2,162 recoveries, a case-fatality rate of 48.4% — covers the DRC's 17th Ebola outbreak, which began in Ituri province in the northeast and has since spread to seven provinces. It is caused by the Bundibugyo strain, for which the proven Ervebo vaccine (established against the more common Zaire strain) is not validated; some 70,000 Ervebo doses were allocated in August and trial doses are now being given to frontline workers. The response is choked by armed-group violence — a transit centre for patients burned on Tuesday 29 September and a case-detection worker was killed — and by displacement, with the UN reporting more than 5,000 people driven from their homes by fighting in Ituri.", "why": "a near-50% fatality rate and a response hamstrung by conflict make this one of the most dangerous Ebola flare-ups in years, and six cases already reported in M23-controlled North Kivu raise the risk of spread beyond government-held areas.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-dr-congo-ebola-toll-passes-4-000", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-02", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Democratic Republic of Congo", "Ebola", "Ituri", "M23"]}
{"id": "2026-10-03-news-brazil-votes-sunday-in-tight-lula-bolsonaro-race", "date": "2026-10-03", "stream": "news", "headline": "Brazil votes Sunday in tight Lula-Bolsonaro race", "summary": "Brazil holds a presidential election on Sunday 4 October pitting Lula against Flávio Bolsonaro in a neck-and-neck race.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/03/brazil-election-lula-bolsonaro-trump", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Brazil holds a presidential election on Sunday 4 October, with President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and challenger Flávio Bolsonaro running neck and neck in the final polls. Lula, 80, of the Workers' Party, is seeking a fourth non-consecutive term; his main opponent, Flávio Bolsonaro, 45, is a senator and son of former president Jair Bolsonaro, who is under house arrest after being convicted of attempting a coup to overturn his 2022 defeat by Lula. If no candidate wins 50% plus one of the votes, a runoff follows on 25 October. Much of the campaign has turned on relations with Washington: Lula has resisted US conditions tied to Brazil's rare-earth and critical minerals and criticised Trump's regional \"war on drugs,\" while Bolsonaro has pledged to align Brazil with it.", "why": "the result will test whether Latin America's largest democracy tilts toward the Trump-aligned bloc that now governs much of the region, with consequences for trade, climate policy and US influence across the continent.", "thread_id": "2026-10-03-news-brazil-votes-sunday-in-tight-lula-bolsonaro-race", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-03", "event_date": "2026-10-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Brazil", "Lula", "Flávio Bolsonaro"]}
